ware/requirements/SW-REQ-260922-HR29.req.yaml","kind":"file","line":87}],"severity":"info","title":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29 (code_changed, preserved): formats, dotfiles, mtime order, missing-path rejection","verification":{"evidence":[{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29","kind":"requirement"}],"method":"review.changes (spec, code-trace, function and call-level requirement impact)","note":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29 is in review.changes (code_changed, matchLevel function).","status":"confirmed"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-file"],"scenario":"omarchy-menu-file \"Pick\" \"$root\" \"jpg\" where $root/a/x.jpg and $root/b/x.jpg are hard links to one inode: base offers both paths, head offers one"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"Re-run locally with bfs and a stub selector: `r/a/x.jpg` and its hard link `r/b/x.jpg` under one root give two rows at the baseline, one row (`r/a/x.jpg`) at 03e6af6a and at cc710e11. The bot's comment on the PR names this case and leaves it as is (both paths hold the same content; the parent folder's identity is not available from one `find -printf`); the author agreed, and 9H5Y's rationale records it. So this is an accepted limitation, not a defect. A test that pins it would make a later change of the key visible. Wording note: 9H5Y says one find call 'cannot tell the parent directories apart'; find can print the parent path, but not whether two parent paths are the same folder, which is the point.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55},{"id":"specs/software/requirements/SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y.req.yaml","kind":"file","line":21},{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"low","title":"Known limitation: same-name hard links in different folders give one row; no test pins it","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Same-name hard links in two folders\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-file","bin/omarchy-transcode"],"scenario":"omarchy-transcode with ~/Videos a symlink to a tree containing a mode-000 subdirectory: the user picks a file, and omarchy-menu-file exits 1 under pipefail so omarchy-transcode aborts"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"Under `set -euo pipefail` the pipeline status is the rightmost non-zero one. `find -H ... 2>/dev/null` exits 1 on Permission denied but still prints the readable rows. The selector returns the pick, the script exits 1, and `bin/omarchy-transcode:169` (`input=$(omarchy-menu-file ...)` under `set -e`) aborts after the pick. This already happens for a real-directory root at the baseline. -H makes it reachable through link roots (e.g. `~/Videos -> /mnt/disk` with a root-owned `lost+found`), which listed nothing before. Re-run locally with bfs at cc710e11: a link root to a tree with a mode-000 directory lists nothing and exits 0 at the baseline; at cc710e11 (as at 03e6af6a) the stub selector gets the pick and the script exits 1. FFTQ's hazard review names it as open and not covered. The PR's interior-loop row covers loops, not permissions.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54},{"id":"bin/omarchy-transcode","kind":"file","line":169},{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-FFTQ","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"medium","title":"Question: can a pick succeed and the script still exit 1 when a followed symlink root contains an unreadable directory?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Unreadable subdirectory under a followed symlink root\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-file"],"scenario":"two files with identical mtime under one root, and aliased roots Pictures->Videos: compare row order and the surviving path spelling at base and head"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"Baseline `sort -rn` reverses the whole-line tiebreak, so equal-mtime rows were ordered by path descending. `sort -t$'\\t' -k2,2rn` applies `r` to the key only, so ties fall back to an ascending whole-line comparison that starts with `D:i`. Re-run locally at cc710e11 with bfs: two equal-mtime files list b, a at the baseline and a, b at head; roots `V:P` and `P:V` (P a link to V) keep `P/c.jpg` in either order, where the baseline listed only `V/c.jpg`. 9H5Y's hazard review says the spelling depends on the sort order of equal rows; HR29 states mtime-descending only. No test pins the spelling the picker returns or the tie order.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"low","title":"Question: which alias spelling survives the dedup, and is the new equal-mtime order wanted?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Equal-mtime ordering and surviving alias spelling\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"claim":{"params":{"relatesTo":["bin/omarchy-menu-file"],"scenario":"omarchy-menu-file \"Pick\" \"$d:$tmp/alias.jpg\" \"jpg\" where alias.jpg is a symlink to $d/p.jpg: baseline offers one row, cc710e11 offers two"},"type":"behavior_hazard"},"detail":"`-H` dereferences every start point. A root that is a link to a matching regular file was not listed at the baseline; it is listed now. cc710e11 strips the first two tab fields before taking the name, so a bare-file root given next to its own folder dedups (re-run: `bf:bf/p.jpg` gives one row). But a link `alias.jpg -> bf/p.jpg` given next to `bf` now gives two rows for one file (`alias.jpg`, `bf/p.jpg`): one row at 03e6af6a, one row (`bf/p.jpg` only) at the baseline. This follows 9H5Y's rule (different name, separate row), but neither the PR nor FFTQ speaks of file roots; FFTQ's hazard review notes the listing. No test covers it.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54},{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55},{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-FFTQ","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"low","title":"Question: is a symlink-to-file root meant to be listed, and as a second row when its name differs from the target's?","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"behavior hazard: settled only by a recorded test outcome","note":"A runtime-behavior question the package's static facts cannot settle; it needs a test. Suggested test: \"Symlink-to-file root next to its target's folder\".","status":"needs_test"}},{"detail":"f9b526b5 (our proof work) rewrote the note (9H5Y line 96): awk keys on `%D:%i` plus the base name taken after the first two tab fields, so a tab in a file name stays in the name; a newline splits a row, as at the base. This matches `$1 \"/\" name` on line 55. Re-run locally at 66150603: `a<TAB>b.jpg` is offered intact and stays a separate row from its hard link `c.jpg`; a newline name splits into fragments at the baseline and at head. One detail the note leaves out: at head the fragments also pass through the dedup, so two different files `d1/a<NL>x.jpg` and `d2/a<NL>x.jpg` give one `x.jpg` fragment where the baseline gave two. Both outputs are unusable at the baseline too, so this is not a defect. The trace and lifecycle records that name head 03e6af6a are history; the cc710e11 re-review follows them.","refs":[{"id":"specs/software/requirements/SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y.req.yaml","kind":"file","line":96},{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55},{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"info","title":"9H5Y's structural hazard note now states the cc710e11 key (resolved)","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"review.compat is `compatible`: test_diff, mcdc_diff (85 -> 87 decisions), removed_behavior and differential were all checked, with 0 findings and 213 inputs over 5 partitions. The configured harness `test/bdiff/harness.mjs` covers only `shell/plugins/menu/MenuModel.js` and `Menu.qml`, so none of those inputs ran this script; `review.behaviorDiff.inventory` is empty. The removed expressions (`find` default -P, `-printf '%T@\\t%p\\n'`, `sort -rn`, `cut -f2-`) were never run base-vs-head by the harness; the questions above are what such a run would settle. test_diff reports no existing test changed; the edits to `menu-file-test.sh` (our proof work) add rows and comments only. Context, not an approval.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":47},{"id":"test/bdiff/harness.mjs","kind":"file"}],"severity":"info","title":"Compatibility 'compatible' carries no differential evidence for bin/omarchy-menu-file","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"`coverage_unmeasured`: `review.diffCoverage.unmeasuredFiles` = [bin/omarchy-menu-file]. The 0 uncovered lines/outcomes says nothing about lines 47-56. The bash coverage target for this script, `menu-bash-file` (proof.yaml:501), is `enabled: false`. `effects_unavailable`: `review.effects` is absent (call-level effects are not analyzed for bash), so no new-call, gained-effect or caller facts exist. Callers found by reading: `bin/omarchy-transcode:169` and `bin/omarchy-games-retro-install:22` (`|| exit 0`); both use the picked name, which is why cc710e11 keeps hard-linked names apart.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54},{"id":"proof.yaml","kind":"file","line":501},{"id":"bin/omarchy-games-retro-install","kind":"file","line":22}],"severity":"info","title":"Changed-code coverage of the script was not measured; effects were not analyzed","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"The script's two self-skips (`no GNU find`, `find lacks -printf`) call base-test.sh `pass` (not `skip`) and exit 0, so the JUnit testcase would record a pass with nothing run. On this run the evidence points to a real run: `menu-file-test.sh` sits in the same suite behind a GNU find -printf gate that records a JUnit skip (proof.yaml:200), and it ran and passed; testResults show 56/56 passed, 0 skipped. Upstream could use `skip` (`# SKIP`), which test/junit/live-session.sh already treats as a skip.","refs":[{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-file-symlink-root-test.sh","kind":"file","line":11},{"id":"proof.yaml","kind":"file","line":200},{"id":"test/junit/live-session.sh","kind":"file","line":129}],"severity":"info","title":"The PR's test passes on a self-skip too (opinion)","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"The body still says 'Dedup rows after `cut -f2-` with `awk '!seen[$0]++'`'. Head cc710e11 keys on device:inode plus the file name before `cut -f3-`, and the same-name hard-link limitation lives only in a PR comment. Worth updating so the merged history describes the shipped mechanism and its limitation.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":52}],"severity":"info","title":"PR description no longer matches the head (opinion)","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"`-printf` was already GNU-only, and `%D`/`%i` are also GNU (both test scripts gate on GNU find). The `checks_skipped` disclosure (13 gates, e.g. documentation_coverage, security_surface_covered) is configuration, not caused by this PR. Any external auditor assessment recorded on this package is advisory: a note, not an approval.","refs":[{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-file-symlink-root-test.sh","kind":"file","line":10},{"id":"documentation_coverage","kind":"check"}],"severity":"info","title":"Pre-existing: GNU find dependency; 13 checks skipped by configuration","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}},{"detail":"Our proof work added `refuse()` to `menu-file-test.sh` and traced it with `# Verifies: SW-REQ-260922-HR29, SW-REQ-261002-FFTQ, SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y` on line 120. review.scope lists it as an added unit with a function-level mapping and no flags. It runs the real script, asserts exit 1, the `Path not found` message, and that the selector is never reached. It is called for a missing path (HR29, line 131), a link root whose target is gone (FFTQ, line 137) and a missing path next to a real root (9H5Y, line 141); these rows are the error_handling witnesses behind the three `observed_pass` cells.","refs":[{"id":"test/shell.d/menu-file-test.sh","kind":"file","line":121},{"id":"SW-REQ-260922-HR29","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-FFTQ","kind":"requirement"},{"id":"SW-REQ-261002-9H5Y","kind":"requirement"}],"severity":"info","title":"The refuse() test helper is traced at function level to all three requirements","verification":{"evidence":[],"method":"untyped finding","note":"No typed claim: this finding is the author's judgement and is not checked against the package.","status":"unverifiable"}}],"packageDigest":"sha256:edbb9a9866f3ed05e4ee2a421e921e6eb4dcee31a5461d13277a6c2a23dce161","packageIdentity":"epkg-9cc222eeac7d96cc0a9ef7f64a3c13fbbe4a3256c1a4ec90ba9dcadf8fbedfc8","riskAssessment":{"level":"medium","rationale":"A small, well-motivated pipeline change, evaluated at upstream head cc710e11. Its purpose is codified (FFTQ and 9H5Y, which now state the cc710e11 head, key and the same-name hard-link limitation) and observed passing, with witnesses that fail on the baseline; the PR's new hard-link row fails on the previous head. Coverage of the script was not measured, effects were not analyzed, and the differential harness does not run the script. Untested edges remain: pipefail exit 1 via an unreadable directory under a followed root (named open in FFTQ), tie order and alias spelling, symlink-to-file roots, the pinned same-name limitation."},"schemaVersion":"reqproof.review-annotation.v1","suggestedTests":[{"forClaim":4,"rationale":"Pin the accepted limitation (one row) so a later change of the dedup key is visible; 9H5Y's rationale is the intent.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55}],"title":"Same-name hard links in two folders"},{"forClaim":5,"rationale":"mkdir a mode-000 dir under the link target (run as non-root), pick a file with a stub selector, and assert omarchy-menu-file's exit status (and omarchy-transcode's).","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54},{"id":"bin/omarchy-transcode","kind":"file","line":169}],"title":"Unreadable subdirectory under a followed symlink root"},{"forClaim":6,"rationale":"touch -d the same time on two files and on an aliased pair, then assert row order and which path the stub selector receives, at base and head.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":55}],"title":"Equal-mtime ordering and surviving alias spelling"},{"forClaim":7,"rationale":"Pass a folder and a differently named symlink to a file in it; assert whether the file is offered once or twice.","refs":[{"id":"bin/omarchy-menu-file","kind":"file","line":54}],"title":"Symlink-to-file root next to its target's folder"}],"summary":"**What the PR does.** Upstream #13197 (Bartok9, part of #12665) makes `bin/omarchy-menu-file` list files under symlinked roots, so `omarchy transcode` gets a picker when `~/Videos` is a link. It runs `find -H` (start points followed, links inside a root not descended). It drops repeated rows after the mtime sort, keyed on device:inode plus the file name. Head cc710e11 (pushed by the maintainers' bot, agreed by the author) added the name, so hard links under different names stay separate rows. The PR test has 9 rows. The evaluated tree 66150603 is baseline quattro-proof 87c3ca68, plus the upstream change at cc710e11 (c136e71a), plus our proof work (de7142fe..f9b526b5): SW-REQ-261002-FFTQ and -9H5Y, witnesses in `menu-file-test.sh`, spec reviews, and both requirements updated for cc710e11.\n\n**Is the fix verified?** The package says readiness `ready`; next decision `human_judgement` (acceptance) by a maintainer. Disclosures: 3, all inform (coverage_unmeasured, checks_skipped, effects_unavailable). FFTQ, 9H5Y and HR29 are `observed_pass`; testResults: 56 passed, 0 failed, current. Coverage of the script was not measured and effects were not analyzed. Compatibility is `compatible` (213 inputs, 0 findings), but the differential harness never runs this script. I ran the PR test locally with bfs (GNU-compatible, not GNU find): the new hard-link row fails on 03e6af6a and passes on cc710e11.\n\n**Both requirements now match cc710e11.** 9H5Y says one row per file under the same name, records the same-name hard-link case (two folders, one row) as the PR's known limitation, and its structural note now states the key (device:inode plus base name; a tab stays in the name). FFTQ's rationale names head cc710e11. My two earlier wording findings are resolved; only spec text changed since then.\n\n**Questions for a human.** Can a pick under a followed root holding an unreadable directory still exit 1 under pipefail? Which alias spelling survives, and is the new equal-mtime order wanted? Is a symlink-to-file root meant to be listed, and as a second row when its name differs? No test in the package covers these.\n\n**Traceability.** The script and both tests trace to FFTQ, 9H5Y and HR29. No spec-gap candidates; scope coherence `single`.\n\n**Not caused by this PR.** 13 checks skipped by configuration; the GNU find dependency; newline names split rows; the HR29 timeout debt. This advisory review is not an approval."}
